{"records":[{"id":"yncw8whw7m","title":"Code - An integrative multiparametric approach stratifies distinct phenotypes of blast phase chronic myelomonocytic leukemia with differential maturation status and drug sensitivity profiles","description":"Approximately 30% of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia patients undergo transformation to a chemo-refractory blastic phase (BP-CMML). Seeking novel therapeutic approaches we profiled blast transcriptomes from 42 BP-CMMLs, observing extensive transcriptional heterogeneity and poor alignment to current AML classifications. BP-CMMLs displayed distinctive transcriptomic profiles, including enrichment for quiescence and variability in drug response signatures. Integrating clinical, immunophenotype and transcriptome parameters, RandomForest unsupervised clustering distinguished immature and mature subtypes characterized by differential expression of transcriptional modules, oncogenes, apoptotic regulators and patterns of surface marker expression. Subtypes differed in predicted response to AML drugs, validated ex vivo in primary samples. Iteratively refined stratification resolved a classification structure comprising five subtypes along a maturation spectrum, predictive of response to novel agents including consistent patterns for RTK, CDK, MTOR and MAPK inhibitors. Finally, we generated a clinically-applicable decision tree to stratify BP-CMML with high specificity and sensitivity, and potentially guide personalized drug selection for improved outcomes.\n\n###################\n# Code description:    #\n###################\n\nCodes included within this data repository are to be used for figure reproduction or further exploration.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-15T15:11:02.736Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yncw8whw7m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yncw8whw7m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kristian","last_name":"Gurashi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kiran","last_name":"Batta","orcid":"0000-0002-3494-0979"},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Wiseman","orcid":"0000-0003-0715-1402"}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"The Oglesby Charitable Trust","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Leukemia","Acute Myelogenous Leukemia","Chronic Myelomonocytic Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3vtwfjh2sb","title":"Enhanced SREBP2-driven cholesterol biosynthesis by PKCl/i  deficiency in epithelial intestinal cells promotes aggressive serrated tumorigenesis ","description":"The metabolic and signaling pathways regulating aggressive mesenchymal colorectal cancer (CRC) initiation and progression through the serrated route are largely unknown. Although relatively well characterized as BRAF mutant cancers, their poor response to current targeted therapy, difficult preneoplastic detection, and challenging endoscopic resection makes the identification of their metabolic requirements a priority. Here we demonstrate that the phosphorylation of SCAP by the atypical PKC (aPKC), PKC/ promotes its degradation and inhibits the processing and activation of SREBP2, the master regulator of cholesterol biosynthesis. We show that the upregulation of SREBP2 and cholesterol by reduced aPKC levels is essential for controlling metaplasia and generating the most aggressive cell subpopulation in serrated tumors in mice and humans. Since these alterations are also detected prior to neoplastic transformation, together with the sensitivity of these tumors to cholesterol metabolism inhibitors, our data indicate that targeting cholesterol biosynthesis is a potential mechanism for serrated chemoprevention.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-07T07:23:58.1Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3vtwfjh2sb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3vtwfjh2sb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Moscat","last_name":"Diaz-Meco","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cholesterol","Colorectal Cancer","Therapeutics","Cholesterol Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t9dnd6j3tk","title":"CRISPR/dCas9-based metabolic pathway engineering for the systematic optimization of exopolysaccharide biosynthesis in Streptococcus thermophilus","description":"Supporting information\nCRISPR/dCas9-based metabolic pathway engineering for the systematic optimization of exopolysaccharide biosynthesis in Streptococcus thermophilus","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-12T13:24:14.273Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t9dnd6j3tk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t9dnd6j3tk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Linghui","last_name":"Kong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiqiang","last_name":"Xiong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongjun","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lianzhong","last_name":"Ai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5wnn6pxgf","title":"CRISPR/dCas9-based metabolic pathway engineering for the systematic optimization of exopolysaccharide biosynthesis in Streptococcus thermophilus","description":"Supporting information\nCRISPR/dCas9-based metabolic pathway engineering for the systematic optimization of exopolysaccharide biosynthesis in Streptococcus thermophilus","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-26T14:15:41.595Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5wnn6pxgf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5wnn6pxgf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"LInghui","last_name":"Kong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiqiang","last_name":"Xiong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongjun","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lianzhong","last_name":"Ai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Supplementation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9dhbprzxh2","title":"Part1_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase (FLT)3-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:09:36.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9dhbprzxh2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dhbprzxh2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4m2d88hgz","title":"Part2_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:18.026Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4m2d88hgz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4m2d88hgz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xzjmk67vnc","title":"Part3_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:45.533Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xzjmk67vnc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xzjmk67vnc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fkbrcn84vz","title":"Integrating proteomics and metabolomics to reveal MAT2A for metabolic reprogramming in non-small cell lung cancer cells","description":"These data are the original data from the manuscript entitled \"Integrating proteomics and metabolomics to reveal MAT2A for metabolic reprogramming in non-small cell lung cancer cells\". Below is a summary of the manuscript. Methionine adenosyltransferase 2A (MAT2A) links metabolic reprogramming with epigenetic regulation by generating S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) in the methionine cycle. The pattern of MAT2A regulation in metabolic reprogramming within non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) remains poorly understood. Current MAT2A inhibitors exhibit limited efficacy and additional therapeutic strategies need to be explored. Here, we employed proteomics and metabolomics in NSCLC cell lines treated with siRNA targeting MAT2A or the inhibitor AG-270, complemented by targeted metabolomics, RT-qPCR and ATAC-seq, to systematically characterize MAT2A-rewired metabolic reprogramming and identify potential combination strategies. Both MAT2A knockdown and inhibition significantly disrupt fatty acid biosynthesis, cholesterol metabolism, glycolysis and the transsulfuration pathway. In fatty acid biosynthesis, MAT2A influences the expression of key genes FASN and SCD and the levels of key metabolites palmitic acid (PA) and oleic acid (OA), as exogenous PA reverses the cell growth inhibition caused by AG-270. Furthermore, this metabolic link supports the rationale for combining AG-270 with the FASN inhibitor TVB-2640. In cholesterol metabolism, MAT2A regulates biosynthesis and efflux, and combining AG-270 with a liver X receptor agonist, which facilitates cholesterol efflux, augments anti-tumor efficacy. In energy metabolism, MAT2A influences glycolysis via regulation of HIF1A. The GLUT1 inhibitor which prevents glucose uptake, exhibits a synergetic effect when combined with AG-270. In the transsulfuration pathway, MAT2A transcriptionally regulates CBS, a key enzyme for cysteine biosynthesis. Combination treatments of AG-270 with inhibitors targeting genes upregulated in response to AG-270, such as PHGDH, which produces serine for cysteine biosynthesis, and SLC7A11, which mediates cysteine uptake, exhibit synergetic effects. Overall, our findings identify MAT2A as a critical regulator of metabolism in NSCLC and propose rational combination strategies to enhance MAT2A inhibitor efficacy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-25T09:33:12.735Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fkbrcn84vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fkbrcn84vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"怡敏","last_name":"陈","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"56zh5g7bfw","title":"Detection of continuous hierarchical heterogeneity by single-cell surface antigen analysis in the prognosis evaluation of acute myeloid leukaemia","description":"In this study, we provided a CyTOF analysis pipeline to find out the AML heterogeneity dynamics along the pseudotime. Minute differences in temporal traits among samples could be exhibited on a trajectory, thus providing a new strategy for predicting AML relapse and monitoring drug responses over time scale. Here are mass cytometry datasets without pre-processing.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-02T08:53:39.951Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/56zh5g7bfw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/56zh5g7bfw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Shao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Acute Myeloid Leukemia Differentiation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vcsyd7ns8f","title":"Dataset - An integrative multiparametric approach stratifies distinct phenotypes of blast phase chronic myelomonocytic leukemia with differential maturation status and drug sensitivity profiles","description":"Approximately 30% of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia patients undergo transformation to a chemo-refractory blastic phase (BP-CMML). Seeking novel therapeutic approaches we profiled blast transcriptomes from 42 BP-CMMLs, observing extensive transcriptional heterogeneity and poor alignment to current AML classifications. BP-CMMLs displayed distinctive transcriptomic profiles, including enrichment for quiescence and variability in drug response signatures. Integrating clinical, immunophenotype and transcriptome parameters, RandomForest unsupervised clustering distinguished immature and mature subtypes characterized by differential expression of transcriptional modules, oncogenes, apoptotic regulators and patterns of surface marker expression. Subtypes differed in predicted response to AML drugs, validated ex vivo in primary samples. Iteratively refined stratification resolved a classification structure comprising five subtypes along a maturation spectrum, predictive of response to novel agents including consistent patterns for RTK, CDK, MTOR and MAPK inhibitors. Finally, we generated a clinically-applicable decision tree to stratify BP-CMML with high specificity and sensitivity, and potentially guide personalized drug selection for improved outcomes.\n\n###################\n# Dataset description: #\n###################\n\nData included within this data repository is to be used as input for figure reproduction or further exploration.\nThis dataset includes the following higher order folders:\n   - Clinical: includes clinical data for the LAML TCGA cohort. Other clinical data were not shared due to data protection of patients' privacy. Please consult us if requiring this data.\n   - Genotype: includes tabular data of genotypes for the collated cohorts, including binary mutation matrix and whole cohorts mutation data.\n   - Immunophenotype: includes tabular data with mean fluorescent intensity (MFI) computed for each sample against various markers.\n   - Oncoprint: includes oncoprint input data.\n   - RandomForest: includes RF input data, model and clustering results.\n   - Resources: includes RNAseq annotation file, gene metadata and collection of curated gene pathways.\n   - RNASeq: includes results from downstream analysis of bulk RNAseq data.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-15T15:11:25.729Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vcsyd7ns8f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vcsyd7ns8f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kristian","last_name":"Gurashi","orcid":"0000-0002-5540-3398"},{"first_name":"Kiran","last_name":"Batta","orcid":"0000-0002-3494-0979"},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Wiseman","orcid":"0000-0003-0715-1402"}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"The Oglesby Charitable Trust","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Leukemia","Acute Myelogenous Leukemia","Chronic Myelomonocytic Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":143403,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":157,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":143403,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142619,"restricted":71,"embargoed":713},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2358},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1399},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":697},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":483},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":455},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":341},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":277}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140733},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1914},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":905},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":843},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":539},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":372}]}}